  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nA cold front was moving across northeast Wisconsin early this\nafternoon. Temperatures were in the upper 40s to lower 50s over\nthe far north, while temperatures were around 70 across the Fox\nValley east to the lakeshore. Scattered showers were noted across\nnorth-central and far northeast Wisconsin as of 1 pm. The cold \nfront will continue to move across the area through mid to late \nafternoon while the first wave of showers should move to the east. \nAnother round of showers across Minnesota is anticipated to move\nacross the north later this afternoon into this evening. \n\nBack to reality on Monday with temperatures closer to normal with \nhighs in the upper 40s to middle 50s. The cool down will be brief\nas above normal temperatures return on Tuesday and then continue\ninto next weekend. For Monday night into Tuesday morning, an \nupper level disturbance will pass to the northwest of the area.\nThis feature will bring a chance of showers to the north. Tranquil\nweather is expected Tuesday afternoon and Tuesday night before the\nnext system approaches from the west on Wednesday. This feature is \nmore vigorous and should bring 50-70% chance of rain Wednesday\nnight into Thursday morning. Potential exists for a few\nthunderstorms which are not anticipated to be severe at this time. \nBeyond Thursday, confidence is lower on timing/location of higher\nchances of rain from Friday into the weekend due to model\ndifferences between the GFS/ECMWF/Canadian. Plenty of time to \nsort out the details in the coming day.s\n\nIf you are a fan of cold weather, this week will not be it as\nabove normal temperatures are expected from Tuesday into the next\nweekend. Potential for some locations to reach 70 degrees on\nWednesday. During the El Nino events, October and November are \nlikely to end up warmer than normal based on past events. So \nfar this month, we are averaging 5-8 degrees above normal.",
